# 0003. Only the owner merges to main, enforced by a hook

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

Records an existing decision (accepted by the owner on 2026-11-11): Claude works through
branches and PRs, and a hook makes merging or pushing to `main` impossible for it.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: a merge or push to `main` by Claude would skip my review; instructions alone
  don't stop a model from running the command.
- **Hook**: the `workflow` plugin registers a `PreToolUse` hook in
  `plugins/workflow/hooks/hooks.json` with matcher `Bash|mcp__.*merge_pull_request`, running
  `guard-main.py`. It reads the tool call as JSON on stdin and exits `2` with a reason on
  stderr to block.
- **Blocked**:
  - any MCP tool ending in `merge_pull_request`;
  - `gh pr merge` (with any flags), REST `/pulls/<n>/merge`, GraphQL `mergePullRequest` /
    `enablePullRequestAutoMerge`, and direct ref updates of `main` or `master` through the
    git refs API;
  - `git push` to `main` or `master`: refspecs are parsed (`+src:dst`, `refs/heads/`
    prefix), a bare push or `HEAD` resolves the current branch with
    `git branch --show-current`, `--all` and `--mirror` are refused;
  - pushes it can't analyse safely: a line `shlex` can't parse, or a push inside a nested
    shell (`sh`, `bash`, `zsh`, `eval`, `xargs`).
- **Parsing**: the command is split into simple commands on `;`, `&`, `|`, `(`, `)`; `cd`
  and `git -C` update the working directory used to resolve the branch; git global options
  with arguments (`-c`, `--git-dir`, `--work-tree`, ...) are skipped; `/usr/bin/git` counts
  as `git`. The patterns match the raw command text, so a command that merely quotes them
  (e.g. writing this file through a heredoc) is blocked too.
- **Flow**: changes go through a `<type>/<slug>` branch and a PR (`github-flow`); I merge
  with squash. Tests: `plugins/workflow/hooks/test-guard.sh`, run by `make check`.
- **Consequences**: nothing reaches `main` without me, in every repo, whatever the remote's
  settings. Pushes chained with `&&` or wrapped in shells are refused, so `git push` runs as
  a command of its own.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Claude Code `PreToolUse` hooks, Python 3 stdlib (`shlex`, `re`, `subprocess`),
  git, GitHub CLI.
- Alternatives: instructions only: the model can still run the command; GitHub branch
  protection alone: doesn't cover local repos or repos without it.
